WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.440
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.549
In 2017, scientists at OpenAI published a
paper where virtual humans learned to tackle

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:18.540
each other in a sumo competition of sorts,
and found out how to rock a stable stance

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.590
to block others from tackling them.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.430
This was a super interesting work because
it involved self-play, or in other words,

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:32.870
copies of the same AI were playing against
each other, and the question was, how do we

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.059
pair them with each other to maximize their
learning.

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:41.780
They found something really remarkable when
they asked the algorithm to defeat an older

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:42.780
version of itself.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:50.500
If it can reliably pull that off, it will
lead to a rapid and predictable learning process.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.880
This kind of curriculum-driven learning can
supercharge many different kinds of AIs.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.820
For instance, this robot from a later paper
is essentially blind as it only has proprioceptive

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.290
sensors, which means that the only thing that
the robot senses is its own internal state

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.080
and that’s it.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.700
No cameras, depth sensors, no LIDAR, nothing.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:19.640
And at first, it behaves as we would expect
it…look, when we start out, the agent is

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:26.500
very clumsy and can barely walk through a
simple terrain… but as time passes, it grows

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:32.030
to be a little more confident, and with that,
the terrain also becomes more difficult over

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.140
time in order to maximize learning.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.820
That is a great life lesson right there.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.060
So, how potent is this kind of curriculum
in teaching the AI?

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.840
Well, it learned a great deal in the simulation,
and as scientists deployed it into the real

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:52.600
world, just look at how well it traversed
through this rocky mountain, stream, and not

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.119
even this nightmarish snowy descent gave it
too much trouble.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:03.670
This new technique proposes a similar curriculum-based
approach where we would teach all kinds of

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.340
virtual lifeforms to navigate on stepping
stones.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:15.160
The examples include a virtual human, a bipedal
robot called cassie, and…this sphere with

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.020
toothpick legs too.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.910
The authors call it “monster”, so, you
know what, monster it is.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:27.160
So, the fundamental question here is, how
do we organize the stepping stones in this

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.630
virtual environment to deliver the best teaching
to this AI?

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:37.250
We can freely choose the heights and orientations
of the upcoming steps, and…of course, it

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.330
is easier said than done.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:45.970
If the curriculum is too easy, no meaningful
learning will take place, and if gets too

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:53.560
difficult too quickly, well…then…in the
better case, this happens…and in the worst

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.800
case, whoops!

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.630
This work proposes an adaptive curriculum
that constantly measures how these agents

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:09.490
perform, and creates challenges that progressively
get harder, but in a way that they can be

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:11.660
solved by the agents.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:18.080
It can even deal with cases where the AI already
knows how to climb up and down, and even deal

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.150
with longer steps.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:26.290
But that does not mean that we are done, because
if we don’t build these spirals right, this

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.290
happens.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:33.461
But, after learning 12 to 24 hours with this
adaptive curriculum learning method, they

00:03:33.461 --> 00:03:41.600
become able to even run, deal with huge step
height variations, high step tilt variations,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:48.420
and let’s see if they can pass the hardest
exam…look at this mess, my goodness, lots

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.490
of variation in every parameter.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.490
And…Yes!

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:52.490
It works!

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:59.950
And the key point is that the system is general
enough that it can teach different body types

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.560
to do the same.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.730
If there is one thing that you take home from
this video, it shouldn’t be that it takes

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.130
from 12 to 24 hours.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.740
It should be that the system is general.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:16.650
Normally, if we have a new body type, we need
to write a new control algorithm, but in this

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:22.520
case, whatever the body type is, we can use
the same algorithm to teach it.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.750
Absolutely amazing.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:25.750
What a time to be alive!

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.430
However, I know what you’re thinking.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.830
Why teach them to navigate just stepping stones?

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:38.590
This is such a narrow application of locomotion,
so why this task?

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:42.790
Great question, and the answer is that the
generality of this technique we just talked

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:50.030
about also means that the stepping stone navigation
truly was just a stepping stone, and here

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:56.570
it is - we can deploy these agents to a continuous
terrain and expect them to lean on their stepping

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.280
stone chops to navigate well here too.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.190
Another great triumph for curriculum-based
AI training environments.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:06.150
So what do you think?

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.200
What would you use this technique for?

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:13.210
Let me know in the comments, or if you wish
to discuss similar topics with other Fellow

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:18.950
Scholars in a warm and welcoming environment,
